The GI Bill (1944), which gave US WWII veterans free college, produced the largest expansion of the middle class in American history — direct evidence that free education is economically transformative at national scale.
The 'student loan crisis' is regressive: low-income graduates often pay a higher percentage of lifetime income in debt service than high-income graduates, reversing the redistributive intent of the system.
Countries with free higher education do not have lower quality universities — Sweden's Uppsala, Germany's LMU Munich, Norway's NTNU, and France's École Polytechnique all rank among the world's best.
Financial aid programmes are complex, error-prone, and create information barriers that deter low-income students even when money is formally available — universal free tuition is simpler and more effective.
Free community college in Tennessee (the Tennessee Promise, 2015) increased enrolment by 25%, with the largest gains among low-income and first-generation students.
